WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
How do you measure a whole forest's photosynthesis without sealing it inside a jar?

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.780
Instead, fast sensors on a tower above the canopy watch carbon ride the wind.

3
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.960
That method is eddy covariance.

4
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:22.473
Fast sensors above the canopy track vertical wind and CO2 concentration.

5
00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:28.113
Sinking gusts carry air into the canopy; rising gusts lift it back out.

6
00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:34.393
When rising gusts hold less CO2 than sinking ones, the forest takes carbon in.

7
00:00:35.813 --> 00:00:39.333
Averaged yearly, that gives net ecosystem exchange, NEE.

8
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.887
That net combines two opposite rates.

9
00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:48.927
Gross primary production, GPP, is total photosynthetic fixation.

10
00:00:50.407 --> 00:00:56.147
Ecosystem respiration, Reco, is release from plants, animals, fungi and bacteria.

11
00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:00.507
Suppose the tower counts release to the air as positive.

12
00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:06.207
If a forest stores carbon this year, will its NEE be positive or negative?

13
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:18.550
Negative. With release counted positive, NEE = Reco − GPP.

14
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:25.450
A forest that stores carbon fixes more than it releases, so NEE falls below zero.

15
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:31.610
Say a tower reads minus −300 grams of carbon per square meter per year.

16
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.610
Uptake beat release by 300: a net sink.

17
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.810
300 is net exchange, not photosynthesis.

18
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:46.530
A flux tower measures net exchange alone; a partitioning model splits photosynthesis

19
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:52.970
from respiration. At night, photosynthesis stops, so the tower records respiration

20
00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.850
alone. Models extend night-time respiration into daytime.

21
00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:05.050
Adding modelled respiration to net uptake estimates GPP, which is larger than 300.

22
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:07.633
Two traps mislead.

23
00:02:08.993 --> 00:02:13.553
First, check the sign convention; reversing it flips uptake into release.

24
00:02:14.873 --> 00:02:19.653
Second, no method observes GPP directly at ecosystem scale.

25
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:26.273
Besides night-time extrapolation, models use light-response fits or neural networks.

26
00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:30.353
Every GPP estimate carries a method attached.

27
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:33.313
Towers now span whole networks.

28
00:02:34.633 --> 00:02:40.973
In 2020, a study applied a neural-network method trained on environmental drivers to

29
00:02:40.973 --> 00:02:44.853
36 FLUXNET2015 tower sites.

30
00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:47.713
It split each tower’s net flux.

31
00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:53.533
Yet methods can agree without being right, since none observes GPP directly.

32
00:02:54.733 --> 00:03:01.633
In 2022, Kukka-Maaria Kohonen and colleagues compared four GPP methods at Hyytiälä,

33
00:03:02.013 --> 00:03:07.653
Finland. They studied a Scots pine forest, 2013 to 2017.

34
00:03:09.073 --> 00:03:11.393
Two methods used CO2 fluxes.

35
00:03:12.373 --> 00:03:17.193
Two used carbonyl sulfide, a gas leaves take up alongside CO2.

36
00:03:18.313 --> 00:03:20.393
That gives a second route to GPP.

37
00:03:21.617 --> 00:03:28.197
Daily carbonyl sulfide estimates averaged 7% or 23% % above the CO2 reference.

38
00:03:29.477 --> 00:03:33.437
The figure depended on the model of the two gases’ uptake ratio.

39
00:03:34.737 --> 00:03:37.097
The study does not say which estimate is true.

40
00:03:38.337 --> 00:03:42.537
Trust a flux tower for net exchange, and always name the model behind

41
00:03:42.537 --> 00:03:43.337
photosynthesis.
